docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-413-
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-414-## A1.5 Manifest authority: resolving the circularity
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-415-
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-416-The review surfaced a hole in §4.4 as written: the bundle's issuer-key
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-417-manifest was to carry "timestamped evidence that those keys were authorized
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-418-issuer keys at issue time, the manifest itself anchored." But **anchoring
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-419-proves existence, not authority** — anyone can anchor a manifest claiming
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-420-anything. A manifest signed by the keys it authorizes is circular; a
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-421-manifest signed by a separate root key reintroduces the single trust
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-422-domain §3.1 rejects.
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-423-
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-424-**Resolution (not deferral): apply §3.1's own argument recursively.**
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-425-Authority at issue time cannot be conjured from nothing — every PKI bottoms
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-426-out in trust anchors; the honest move is to make the anchors plural,
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-427-independent, archived, and time-anchored. The manifest carries **two
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-428-external authority-publication evidences plus proof-of-possession**:
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-429-
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-430-1. **DNSSEC chain snapshot** — the `wamason.com` records publishing the
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-431-   issuer-key fingerprints, with the signature chain to the root as it
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-432-   existed at issue time, *archived in the bundle* (discovery channels stay
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-433-   discovery; the archived snapshot is what verification uses).
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-434-2. **Anchored repository publication** — the public git commit publishing
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-435-   the same fingerprints, with the commit object, its signature, the
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-436-   relevant proof material, and the OTS proof **archived in the bundle**
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-437-   (like the DNSSEC snapshot: verification uses the archive, never live
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-438-   repository availability — P9).
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-439-3. **Manifest self-signature** by the issuer keys — *proof of possession
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-440-   only*. It binds the manifest bytes to the asserted keys and is never
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-441-   counted as authority evidence (P10): an attacker's invented key
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-442-   self-signs for free.
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-443-
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-444-`VALID_STRICT` requires both external evidences plus possession. A future
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-445-verifier sees that at issue time, two channels with independent operational
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-446-failure modes asserted the same key authority, and the asserted keys were
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-447-demonstrably held. Subverting the process requires tampering with both
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-448-external channels, at or before issue time, consistently.
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-449-
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-450-**Declared residual risks (difficulty, not impossibility):**
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-451-
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-452-- **Correlated control.** Both external channels are ultimately operated by
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-453-  the same author — same registrar sphere, same person. Their independence
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-454-  is real against *channel failure* (registrar lapse vs. repository-host
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-455-  lapse) and against an attacker who compromises one channel; it is weaker
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-456-  against an adversary who owns the author's entire operational sphere at
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-457-  issue time. That adversary sits at the misissuance boundary (A1.3, item
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-458-  7) and is met operationally, not cryptographically.
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-459-- **Trust-anchor recursion.** The archived DNSSEC chain validates against
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-460-  the root trust anchors *as they existed at issue time*. A verifier
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-461-  decades later needs trustworthy knowledge of those historical anchors —
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-462-  which is itself a historical-authority question. Concretely, the
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-463-  reference verifier maintains an **archived historical trust-anchor
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-464-  store** (DNS root keys and repository signing keys, by validity period),
--
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-507-  proof-of-work history remains intact and available over the
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-508-  adjudication horizon**. A
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-509-  fifty-year adjudication story rests on Bitcoin's fifty-year survival;
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-510-  stating that plainly is the declare-what-you-cannot-see ethos, applied
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md-511-  to the anchor itself.
