docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-30-## What this amendment establishes
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-31-
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-32-**The claim.** V1 is a dual-signed timestamp notary for Tessera's own domain, `wamason.com`. Provided the service's
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-33-signing capability was not misused within the key's window, a V1 receipt lets anyone, with no network and no help
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-34-from Tessera, check four things: that particular bytes were presented to the Tessera V1 service; that both of the
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-35-service's keys signed them; that two channels (DNSSEC, and a signed, timestamped git commit) said those keys
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-36-belonged to that domain and were allowed to issue during a stated window; and that the bytes *and both signatures*
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-37-existed no later than a time T fixed by Bitcoin. Against the window, the declared issue time is not earlier than
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-38-its not-before, and the verified chain-time T is not later than its not-after.
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-39-
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-40-**The adversary.** Anyone who tampers with, forges, strips, swaps, replays, reattributes or backdates receipts;
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-41-anyone who captures authority-channel credentials while signing capability stays uncompromised (A8.3's sense of
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-42-capture); and anyone who steals a key, gains signing capability, or breaks a signature algorithm *after* a receipt
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-43-was anchored. Against these, V1's verdicts hold under the registered cryptographic, key-use and chain assumptions.
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-44-
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-45-**The boundaries.** V1 does not say who submitted the bytes or what they mean. It does not evaluate revocation, or
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-46-whether an algorithm or key was still safe when it signed. It offers no standing, renewal, wrappers or degraded
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-47-verification, and no lower time bound or priority. It does not hold against an adversary who holds signing
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-48-capability for every required slot inside the window (capture of the issuing AWS account confers it for both
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-49-slots, C3); against whole-author-sphere capture (R-2.15); or against breaks of SHA-256, OpenTimestamps, Bitcoin
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-50-history deeper than k, or, in git's links, a second-preimage break of SHA-1 or attacker influence over committed
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-51-contents; or against compromise of the verifier's distribution, meaning its release and the trust configuration
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-52-compiled into it (an explicit external assumption, A9.5). Both keys are under one controller.
