docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:253:## A9.4 Revocation and historical security: not evaluated — AUTHOR DECISION B; OWNER DECISION for historical security. A weakening under A1 §A1.1.
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-254-
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-255-- **Weakened.** For V1 bundles, `VALID_STRICT` evaluates neither P6 condition: `key_authorized(declared_issue_time)`
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-256-  against effective revocation, and no revocation effective at or before `max(anchor_time, declared_issue_time)`
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-257-  (A4.3). Every report carries `revocation: NOT_EVALUATED` next to the verdict. *Why:* under the registered text
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-258-  a verifier that cannot evaluate revocation returns `UNVERIFIABLE` for every receipt.
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-259-- **Historical security.** Every report carries `historical_security: NOT_EVALUATED`. V1 does not evaluate whether
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-260-  a signing algorithm or key was safe when it signed. The documentation names what a later examiner must supply:
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-261-  an authenticated safe-before cutoff per algorithm and per key, compared against T with A2.1's chain-time
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-262-  uncertainty (CODEX-REVIEW 8). A surviving PQ attestation slot prevents a P-256 break alone from supplying every
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-263-  required attestation signature. An ML-DSA-65 cutoff and the classical slot remain; historical authority, per-key
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-264-  custody and continuing commitment and history security remain assumptions. A PQ slot never removes the need for
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-265-  a theft cutoff.
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-266-- **Issuer duty.** The V1 issuer never re-authorizes a key (R-5.24). V1 defines no revocation publication.
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-267-- **Contract for later verifiers.** Missing status capture does not by itself invalidate a V1 receipt. A later
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-268-  verifier given a V1 bundle, under the same governing V1 specification, policy, trust configuration and other
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-269-  declared inputs, returns the same verdict and values in the stable field set (A9.1), and never a weaker verdict
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-270-  because of missing status capture. **Verdict stability** (OWNER DECISION 2026-10-13, `docs/V1-V2-COMPATIBILITY.md`): a V1
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-271-  bundle is always evaluated under its signed `common.spec`. Trust configurations are append-only (A9.5), so a
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-272-  later configuration cannot turn a V1 verdict into `UNVERIFIABLE` by dropping a KSK, a header pin or a repository
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-273-  key. Later algorithm policy enters the separate historical-security assessment only as an authenticated
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-274-  per-algorithm cutoff compared with T, with A2.1's chain-time uncertainty; authenticated per-key theft cutoffs are
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-275-  assessed separately there as well. It never overwrites the V1 verdict or its six stable fields, whether T is
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-276-  before, equal to or after a cutoff. A receipt whose T precedes an algorithm's cutoff keeps its V1 verdict; that
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-277-  comparison alone does not establish historical safety. Later revocation, standing, renewal and historical-security
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-278-  results are reported separately, together with later adverse evidence. The V1 verdict continues to apply the
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-279-  algorithm policy registered for its governing V1 specification, including its original malformed, unsupported and
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-280-  prohibited-identifier cases (A9.7 row 29). This
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-281-  binds later verifiers and profiles; it is not a property V1 itself demonstrates. **This is not
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-282-  non-repudiation.** Evidence (C7): for an otherwise valid, fully performable bundle where missing status capture
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-283-  is the sole difference, a companion returning `INVALID` or `UNVERIFIABLE` must go red.
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-284-
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-285-## A9.5 Strict-only, standalone; the receipt anchor — OWNER DECISIONS 2026-10-12 except where marked
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-286-
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-287-- **Dispatch.** Out-of-profile input gives `UNVERIFIABLE / OUT_OF_PROFILE` at dispatch; A4.2 precedence holds inside.
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-288-- **H1a's suite.** Forge, tamper, backdate, break-chain, strip-one-signature, out-of-profile, the A9.1 mapping.
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-289-  Degradation and wrapper instances move to their capabilities.
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-290-- **Receipt anchor.** OTS stamps `SHA256(A_sig)`, tag `tessera-v1/receipt-anchor-sig`, which commits to both frames
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-291-  and their exact detached signatures in manifest slot order (ENCODING-V1 §5, D-8, D-14). The verifier verifies the
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-292-  committed signature bytes only. The frame-only layout is withdrawn, with no fallback.
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-293-- **Issue order (D-15).** Fix manifest and metadata; build frames; sign; verify; **freeze** the signature bytes;
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-294-  **stamp** `A_sig` (one stamp per receipt, R-3.42); obtain confirmation; ship. A signature made after T cannot fit
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-295-  the anchor. A retry that changes any committed byte needs a fresh stamp. Signature identifiers stay independent
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-296-  of signature bytes; the anchor digest may commit to them; nothing is normalized after stamping.
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-297-- **Git stamp target (D-16).** The git channel's OTS stamp targets the **signed commit's object id**, never a tree,
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-298-  blob or unsigned commit. The verifier recomputes the id, verifies `gpgsig`, follows commit→tree→blob, and checks
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-299-  the stamped id. SHA-1 linkage remains a stated residual. The git evidence names its object format, and V1
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-300-  accepts only `sha1`: the key-assertion repository stays in SHA-1 format at a host that supports it, whatever
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-301-  git's default becomes (the author, 2026-10-13: Git 3 moves to SHA-256 in 2027; GitHub does not support it).
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-302-  The pre-freeze bundle schema (V1-PROFILE §2.1, G-26) defines the git-evidence member `object_format` as the string
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-303-  `"sha1"`. It is an evidence descriptor, not a new signed-frame or key-publication field. A missing descriptor gives
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-304-  `UNVERIFIABLE / GIT_OBJECT_FORMAT_MISSING`; an unsupported value gives `UNVERIFIABLE / GIT_OBJECT_FORMAT`. The
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-305-  verifier recomputes the SHA-1 object ids from the raw commit/tree/blob bytes, so this descriptor cannot override
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-306-  their interpretation. For V1 the stamped message is exactly 40 lowercase ASCII hex digits naming the signed commit
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-307-  id followed by one LF byte. The schema/check specification and conformance cases are fixed before the 30 Oct
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-308-  profile freeze; no P8 frame or key-publication vector changes are required.
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-309-- **Root-KSK rule.** The root DNSKEY set is valid at the snapshot's anchored time **if some trusted KSK whose reliance
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-310-  period contains that time validates it**. Each KSK entry records signing-start, signing-end and a sourced reliance
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-311-  lifetime L, with the reliance interval [signing-start, signing-end + L]. L is the registered acceptance-policy
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-312-  value derived from the cited root DNSKEY RRSIG validity evidence; a largest observed lifetime is labelled observed,
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-313-  not asserted to be a proven global maximum. A candidate must also satisfy cryptographic validation and the selected
--
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-342-  (SPIKE-REPORT, KSK-2024 re-capture; without it every receipt from 2026-10-11 would be falsely rejected).
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-343-- **Custody.** V1 offers no renewal. Long-horizon validity depends on the dependency manifest and on the holder
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-344-  keeping the exact bytes (a mirror that alters a signature byte breaks its copy, CODEX-REVIEW 5).
