docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-375-- **Slot-2 custody (OWNER DECISION 2026-10-13).** The `gpg`-slot key is generated non-exportable in hardware outside
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-376-  the issuing AWS account: a token on author-controlled hardware, or an HSM-backed key at a provider other than AWS.
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-377-  The owner chooses between them, and records the choice, dated, in the profile before the 30 Oct freeze. The choice
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-378-  takes into account measured token throughput and issuance availability, and the author's concern about scalability
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-379-  (2026-10-13: *"Scalability is a real, legitimate, concern"*). Either choice satisfies the author's key-protection
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-380-  requirement (A9.0) and leaves C1–C7 unchanged. The key's authority is evidenced by the key-publication record in
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-381-  both cases.
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-382-  - *Policy signer.* In either custody option, a queue-pulling policy signer outside the issuing AWS account
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-383-    validates the complete V1 frame and enforces the declared-time check using its own clock before requesting a
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-384-    signature. The issuing AWS account can enqueue frames but cannot invoke the slot-2 key directly, change that
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-385-    policy or clock, or obtain the policy signer's key-use credentials. The slot-2 key is dedicated to V1 tagged
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-386-    type-0x00 documents (attestation and manifest-possession). The dated custody record names this enforcement
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-387-    boundary and its access policy. Non-exportability protects against key export, not signing misuse through the
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-388-    permitted queue path.
