docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-430-
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-431-**The two product guarantees, stated conditionally (CODEX-REVIEW 7).** Guarantee 2 is the C4 window rule, stated
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:432:as a property; a finding against it is a C4 finding for the closure rule (A9.2). Guarantee 1 is not computed.
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-433-1. **Past receipts survive later compromise.** A receipt whose exact signatures are anchored by T cannot have
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-434-   signatures manufactured after an adversary gains signing capability for every required slot substituted into
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-435-   it, while SHA-256, the OTS commitment and the pinned chain history stay secure. Its historical authenticity also
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-436-   needs the signing and key-use assumptions to have held before T, authentic historical authority, and no relevant
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-437-   compromise before T; V1 does not evaluate these (`historical_security: NOT_EVALUATED`). A later key theft or
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-438-   algorithm break does not by itself invalidate such a receipt. It does not cure earlier theft, unauthorized
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-439-   signing before T, misissuance or whole-author-sphere capture, and does not prove that the issuer itself obtained
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-440-   the stamp or issued the bundle uniquely.
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-441-2. **Signing capability mints only inside the window, in chain time.** Even signing capability for every required
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-442-   slot cannot make a receipt pass V1 with verified T after its authenticated not-after, under continuing anchor
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-443-   and history assumptions and provided the authority channels are not also captured (republishing a window is a
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-444-   new authority event; whole capture is R-2.15). Translating this into a finite UTC cutoff requires an additional,
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-445-   explicit backward-skew assumption that V1 does not establish: A2.1 registers no finite bound on B − C, and
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-446-   neither ε nor S is one. Inside the window, such an adversary mints receipts that pass; the window cannot be
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-447-   shortened, and detecting misuse is the B(ii) add-on, not V1.
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-448-
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-449-**C3 non-claim.** The two keys are *separate technical failure domains under a single controller; no separation of
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-450-control or independent fates.* The `gpg` signer signs frames delivered by the AWS-side issuer, so capture of the
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-451-issuing account confers signing capability for both slots; the non-exportable key prevents key export, not misuse.
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-452-With the hybrid the slots differ in algorithm (lattice and elliptic curve) and custody (AWS KMS and the
docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md-453-slot-2 custody); under the fallback a P-256 break defeats both slots.
